## Ancestor board unavailable

The optional upgrade-service Git reader uses only local committed history and never writes Git or database state, fetches, or prompts. Missing `ORIG_HEAD` or upstream, an absent committed `.mtix/tasks.json`, multiple bases, an unsupported Git installation or platform, invalid board data, or time/output limits produce an explained unavailable result. The supported layout is an ordinary self-contained repository: `.git/shallow` and `.git/objects/info/alternates` each cause conservative refusal even when a base is readable. Borrowed-object graphs can be complete; the refusal is a supported-layout boundary, not a claim that their ancestry is always incomplete. Git-less and mgit-only checkouts fall back cleanly. This reader provides provenance for a later import loss check; it does not change the current import recovery procedure. Keep a refused incoming board and follow the command's printed recovery guidance; do not override refusals or run a repository-provided Git executable.

## Received provenance has pending or unknown outbound work

A hub receipt is published only when the completed pull observes no pending outbound journal events, including held push events, in the same board snapshot. Pending work preserves the prior receipt (or its absence) and reports `pending-outbound`. Run `mtix sync push`, resolve any held work it reports, then run a new, complete `mtix sync pull`; push alone does not publish a receipt or clear that warning. Older hub receipts whose outbound boundary is absent or null remain readable, but status and doctor infer `outbound-boundary-unknown` without rewriting them. This is historical uncertainty, not a recorded failed exchange; a later saved omission takes precedence. A fresh receipt with known zero replaces that uncertainty only after a new eligible pull.

Held push events remain pending until resolved. Use `mtix sync quarantine list` and the recovery the push prints; never delete journal rows, edit receipt hashes or rewrite local metadata to obtain a zero boundary. Other saved omission categories retain their existing meaning and may be stale after diagnostics failed to clear.

## Named lock acquisition or dispatch contention

Push and dispatch coordination use `.mtix/data/sync.push.lock` and `.mtix/data/dispatch.lock`. Both require a real data directory and regular lock file owned by the effective OS user; symlinked directories or lock files, Windows reparse points and wrong object types are refused. Do not remove, replace or unlink a lock file to bypass its owner. If acquisition is refused, stop project writers and ask the operator to restore a real, correctly owned local data directory without discarding its data.

Each daemon pass takes `dispatch.lock` before pending-mirror recovery, hub pull, relay and hook dispatch, then releases it before waiting. A contended pass remains alive and retries at its existing cadence; it performs none of that work while another dispatcher holds the pass lock. This differs from a duplicate daemon start, which remains a clean no-op under the separate lifetime `sync.daemon.lock`. The legacy `mtix sync daemon --dispatch-hooks` spelling uses the same pass lock; the daemon without that flag still locks its pull/relay pass.

`mtix sync doctor` reports the local `data filesystem` check in text and JSON. Known network or FUSE mounts produce a passing WARN with local-disk recovery guidance; a failed or unavailable classifier also warns without claiming reliable locking. Only recognized local disk filesystem names (apfs on macOS; ext, xfs, btrfs and similar on Linux) pass; any other name also warns, because an unrecognized filesystem is not a locking guarantee. On Windows the filesystem cannot be classified, so this row always shows a WARN (classification unavailable); it cannot be cleared, it does not fail the doctor, and it does not mean the store is unsafe. The check performs no network contact of its own; the doctor's existing hub checks still contact the hub and can fail independently. Existing unsafe-filesystem write refusal remains in force. Give each machine its own local `.mtix` and use the hub to share changes.

## Common Errors

### Pull sync lock is busy

A contended local sync lock makes ordinary `mtix sync pull` fail. With `MTIX_SYNC_HOOK=1`, native lock contention instead warns that the pull was not performed and returns success. Retry after the current owner finishes; no pull was completed or automatically retried. Filesystem acquisition failures and cancellation remain errors. Never delete the sync lock file to bypass its owner.

### ErrNotFound

**Cause:** The node ID does not exist or has been soft-deleted.

**Solution:** Verify the ID with `mtix show <id>`. If soft-deleted, use `mtix undelete <id>`.

`mtix undelete` restores only what the same delete removed: descendants deleted separately stay deleted. After `mtix rerun --strategy delete`, undelete each direct child of the rerun task: one undelete of a child restores that child and the descendants the rerun deleted beneath it. Other direct children keep their own recovery, and restored nodes stay `invalidated` until you run `mtix restore`.

### ErrInvalidTransition

**Cause:** The requested status change is not allowed by the state machine.

**Solution:** Check [STATUS_MACHINE.md](STATUS_MACHINE.md) for valid transitions. Some transitions require specific commands (e.g., `reopen`, `unclaim`).

### ErrAlreadyClaimed

**Cause:** Another agent has already claimed this node.

**Solution:** Use `mtix show <id>` to see the current assignee. Wait for them to release it or use force-reclaim if the agent is stale.

### ErrNodeBlocked

**Cause:** The node has unresolved blocking dependencies.

**Solution:** Use `mtix blocked <id>` to see blockers. Resolve them first.

### ErrCycleDetected

**Cause:** Adding this dependency would create a circular dependency chain.

**Solution:** Review the dependency graph and restructure to avoid cycles.

### ErrNoActiveSession

**Cause:** Attempted an agent operation without an active session.

**Solution:** Start a session first: `mtix session start --agent <id>`.

## Automatic import of `.mtix/tasks.json`

### Auto-import of .mtix/tasks.json refused

**Cause:** After a `git pull` or checkout, `.mtix/tasks.json` lacks local node or dependency data that the automatic (replace) import would delete: a task, a comment (annotation), a comment's resolution, an activity entry or a dependency, or a field value the file leaves empty in a copy of a task that is not current (the local task has no activity, it lacks some of the task's local activity or its `updated_at` is older: a stale board, or one re-exported by an mtix client older than 0.5.4, which writes no comments or activity). A field a teammate cleared on purpose (unclaim, reopen, undefer, undelete, a later update) is not a loss and applies. On a local task with no activity, an equal or later file timestamp cannot prove the teammate saw a local-only edit: a non-empty prompt (or another field) the file leaves empty is reported as a loss. This can also refuse an intentional clear on such a task; merge keeps the listed local value, while explicit replace takes the file. A teammate's dependency removal applies when the file's source task is current and has a new system activity entry for removal of that target and type; a stale board or an already-seen removal entry is insufficient (MTIX-107.44). Nothing was imported and nothing changed; the refusal repeats, printed once, on every command until you choose, and writing commands save to the local store but leave `.mtix/tasks.json` as it is.

**After upgrading from 0.5.3 or earlier:** A board written by 0.5.3 or earlier cannot carry comments, activity or the fields 2.0.0 added (previous status, estimates, who deleted a task, metadata and the rest), so a teammate's newer board always lacks yours. mtix does not count that as a loss. Before it refuses an import, it looks for the board you and your teammate last exchanged: first the last-exchanged record (`.mtix/data/exchange-record.json`, written after each import by the release that adds it and later ones; a board last imported by 0.5.4 or earlier has none), then the git merge-base board (the committed `.mtix/tasks.json` that `ORIG_HEAD` and the branch's upstream both descend from). When that board is in the 1.x form, mtix decides task by task and field by field. A value unchanged here since the exchange takes the file's value (the teammate's change, a claim included). A value unchanged in the file keeps your change, so an unpushed pre-upgrade edit survives. Comments, activity and 2.0.0-only fields the older form could not carry are kept, and so are tasks you made since. The import prints which source it used and what it kept. It still refuses, and lists the task, when both sides changed the same field (`changed both here and in the file since the last exchange`), when the file lacks a task you hold, when a dependency between tasks you both already had differs in either direction (an older board holds none, so mtix cannot tell who changed it; a dependency of a task you made since, or of a task new in the file, is not a difference, because its author is known), or when neither source exists. Where the other task was renumbered on the other side, the import is still refused; run `mtix import .mtix/tasks.json --mode merge` from the project root, the way out the refusal names. A refusal with no source names what was missing and why, and the choices are the ones above (merge, `mtix sync --fix`, replace). Nothing is deleted by the refusal. `mtix sync` shows it again.

**Solution:** Read the list the refusal prints, then run one of these from the project root:

- `mtix import .mtix/tasks.json --mode merge` backs up the database, keeps everything the refusal lists (every local task, comment, activity entry and dependency, and every listed field value, with the task's local status when the value is part of it) and adds the file's changes. Where a task's status, assignee, agent state, wake time or deletion state differs from the file's, it writes nothing and lists each conflict (yours, the file's, an activity hint) until you rerun it with `--prefer theirs|ours`, or `--theirs ID,ID` and `--ours ID,ID`; the choice is recorded in the task's activity. Where they agree, the other field values are decided per task by its content (title, description, prompt, acceptance, labels): where it matches the file's, the local values win; where it differs, the file's copy wins, so your own edits to that task (a prompt edit, for example) are undone. Check `git log -p .mtix/tasks.json` afterwards and reapply what it undid. A task listed as `a different task under this id` is renumbered, with its subtree, to the next number free in both the store and the file (it keeps its uid; the file's task keeps the id); the merge prints the renumbering and applies it only when you rerun it with `--confirm`. A task listed as `a task under this id with a different title and no uid to compare` (the file, from a client at 0.3 or older or never re-exported after an upgrade, holds its id with another title, and either copy has no uid) is taken for a different task: the merge renumbers yours with `--confirm`; if a teammate only retitled it, the merge keeps both copies, and the renumbered one holds your comments, activity and field values the board lacked: move them to the task at the id, or keep the renumbered copy, before you delete anything. For a task listed as `treated as the same task (uid assigned at upgrade)` (the file holds its id under another uid, one of them assigned when a clone upgraded, and the titles differ) the merge takes the file's uid, title and content. If the two titles name different tasks (tasks created in the same second whose create event at least one clone's event log lacks, for example created before 0.2), copy yours to a new task first (`mtix show <id>`, then `mtix create` with its title and description; writes stay local while the refusal is pending), then merge.
- `mtix sync --fix` keeps the local store and rewrites `.mtix/tasks.json` from it; every change in the file is dropped.
- `mtix import .mtix/tasks.json --mode replace` makes the file win and deletes what the refusal listed. It needs the ticket count typed at an interactive terminal (no flag supplies it, and piped input is refused), so an agent never runs it: ask the user; choose the merge or `mtix sync --fix` first. It writes a verified snapshot first, and `mtix backup <file>` is an extra copy.

Each of them resolves the refusal. `mtix sync` shows the last refusal, its kind, its reason and whether it is pending (`--json`: `auto_import.last_refusal`). Every automatic import that does apply prints one line saying what changed, and keeps a backup of the database before it in `.mtix/data/backups/pre-sync-<time>.db` (the newest 5 are kept; a file that fails a check or is refused takes no backup).

### .mtix/tasks.json was not rewritten

**Cause:** A write found `.mtix/tasks.json` changed on disk since mtix last wrote or imported it, and the automatic import did not import it. mtix never overwrites such a board, from the CLI or from `mtix mcp`, `mtix serve` or the daemon; the write is saved in the local store.

**Solution:** Follow the way out the line names for the kind of refusal (`mtix sync` shows it): a lossy file, the three commands above; a conflict (both changed), `mtix import .mtix/tasks.json --mode merge` (the merge writes nothing while a task's status, assignee, agent state, wake time or deletion state differs from the file's: it lists each conflict, and you rerun it with `--prefer theirs|ours`, or `--theirs ID,ID` and `--ours ID,ID`, which it records in the task's activity; where they agree, your other field values win when the task's content (title, description, prompt, acceptance, labels) matches the file's, and otherwise the file's copy wins, undoing your edits to that task; afterwards check `git log -p .mtix/tasks.json` and reapply what it undid), `mtix sync --fix` or, as the last resort (typed ticket count at a terminal), `--mode replace` (when the replace would also delete local data, the conflict is printed as a refusal that lists it), or, if you changed nothing locally, the steps under "A conflict although nothing changed locally" below; a newer schema, upgrade mtix (do not rewrite the board from this older version); a file that fails its checks (for example merged by hand), repair and check it, then `mtix import .mtix/tasks.json --recompute-checksum`, or `mtix sync --fix` to keep the local board (it works even when the file does not parse); automatic import off, `mtix import .mtix/tasks.json --mode merge` (with the same caveat) or `mtix sync --fix`.

### A conflict although nothing changed locally

**Cause:** After an upgrade from mtix 0.5.3 or earlier, the older version's last automatic import was not followed by a writing command, so the conflict baseline (`.mtix/data/sync-db.sha256`) it recorded predates that import. The first pulled `.mtix/tasks.json` is then reported as a conflict (both changed) although you changed nothing.

**Solution:** From the project root, run `mtix sync --fix` (it records a new baseline and rewrites `.mtix/tasks.json` from the local store), then `git checkout HEAD -- .mtix/tasks.json` (it puts the pulled board back), then any command that imports, such as `mtix list`. That command imports the board and prints its one-line notice, or refuses it and lists what it would delete, as above. Never stop after `mtix sync --fix`: alone, it drops every change in the file. Do not use `mtix import .mtix/tasks.json --mode replace` (it asks for the ticket count, typed at an interactive terminal, so it cannot run unattended), which runs no loss check, or the merge. The merge decides in two steps. First, per task, it compares status, assignee, agent state, wake time and deletion state with the file's; progress is not compared, and when the merge changes a task's status, deletion or progress it recalculates the ancestors' progress in the same transaction. Where any differ, the merge guesses nothing about who changed what (several writes leave no activity entry: `mtix update --assignee`, automatic blocking and unblocking, changes applied from the hub): it writes nothing, takes no backup, and lists each conflicting task with the field, your value, the file's value and a hint from the two activity streams (a hint, never a verdict). Rerun it with `--prefer theirs` (take the file's values for every listed task) or `--prefer ours` (keep yours), or per task with `--theirs ID,ID` and `--ours ID,ID`, which win over `--prefer`; a conflicting task that no flag covers still refuses, and an id with no conflict is rejected. The choice moves the task's whole status group together (status, previous status, progress, closed time, wake time, assignee, agent state), is applied in one transaction after the usual backup, and is recorded in the task's activity as a system entry by `import`. The flags apply to `--mode merge` only. Second, where those values agree, the content decides, as before: when your copy's content (title, description, prompt, acceptance and labels) matches the file's, every other local field value stays; when it differs, the file's copy wins, so your own edits to that task (a prompt edit, for example) are undone, except values the file leaves empty in a copy that is not current. Comments and activity entries from both sides are kept. After a merge, check `git log -p .mtix/tasks.json` and reapply what it undid. An automatic three-way merge that uses the board at git's merge base as the common ancestor, so that only true conflicts need a choice, is planned for 0.6.0.

### `sync.auto_sync`

`sync.auto_sync` (default `true`) controls only the automatic import of a changed `.mtix/tasks.json`. `mtix config set sync.auto_sync false` turns it off; `mtix config set` accepts only true or false. A value written by hand that is neither leaves automatic import on, with a one-line warning; fix it with `mtix config set sync.auto_sync true` or `false`. `.mtix/config.yaml` is tracked in git, so the switch applies to every clone, but a store that holds no tasks (a fresh clone) is always imported. With the switch off, writes keep exporting the store, but a pulled board is not overwritten: it stays until you import it with `mtix import .mtix/tasks.json --mode merge` (the merge writes nothing while a task's status, assignee, agent state, wake time or deletion state differs from the file's: it lists each conflict, and you rerun it with `--prefer theirs|ours`, or `--theirs ID,ID` and `--ours ID,ID`, which it records in the task's activity; where they agree, your other field values win when the task's content (title, description, prompt, acceptance, labels) matches the file's, and otherwise the file's copy wins, undoing your edits to that task; afterwards check `git log -p .mtix/tasks.json` and reapply what it undid) or keep your board with `mtix sync --fix`. It does not affect hub sync (`mtix sync push`, `mtix sync pull`). `mtix sync` shows the configured value.

## File relay (experimental)

The file relay (`sync.relay.dir`) is experimental and opt-in. Run `mtix sync doctor`: with the relay configured it adds the `relay reachable`, `relay record`, `relay key`, `relay publishing`, `relay peers`, `relay poll cadence`, `relay snapshots` and `relay privacy` rows, and a failing row prints a `fix:` line where one exists (otherwise the error alone). The `mtix-sync` skill has the symptom table (`RELAY_GAP`, `RELAY_KEY_ABSENT`, `RELAY_PUBLISHER_DIVERGED` and the others) and the recovery commands; run a recovery only when the user asks for it.

